{% comment %}
  The reviews UI itself, shared by both entry points.

  Cited ships two blocks that render exactly the same thing:

    blocks/reviews.liquid       — an app block. The merchant places it inside
                                  their product section. Exact placement, and
                                  no JavaScript is involved at any point.
    blocks/reviews-embed.liquid — an app embed. One toggle in Theme settings
                                  and it is on for every product, with no
                                  placement step and nothing a theme can
                                  refuse. Shopify injects it at the end of
                                  <body>, so a small script moves it into
                                  position.

  They live in one snippet because they had already started to drift: `author`
  on the standard metaobject is a customer reference, not a name, and fixing
  that in one copy while the other kept writing a person's name into a
  reference field is precisely the bug that made syndication fail silently for
  every review ever submitted.

  Parameters are passed explicitly — `render` does not inherit outer scope —
  so this snippet cannot accidentally depend on which block called it.

  Data source: the standard `product_review` metaobject, plus the
  Shopify-mandated `reviews.rating` / `reviews.rating_count` product
  metafields that approved review apps must maintain.

  CSS is scoped entirely under .cited-reviews and driven by custom properties.
  There are NO global selectors and NO element resets — the most common
  competitor complaint is a review widget breaking the theme's buy buttons
  (PLAN.md §2, W1). This cannot reach outside itself.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign rating_field = product.metafields.reviews.rating
  assign count_field = product.metafields.reviews.rating_count

  assign rating_avg = 0
  if rating_field != blank and rating_field.value != blank
    assign rating_avg = rating_field.value.rating | times: 1.0
  endif

  assign rating_count = 0
  if count_field != blank
    assign rating_count = count_field.value | plus: 0
  endif

  # Shopify's `reviews` namespace holds ONLY rating and rating_count - there is
  # no product-to-reviews link in it. This previously read
  # `reviews.product_reviews`, a path that does not exist on any store, so the
  # block could render an average and never a single review.
  #
  # Cited maintains the link itself: an app-owned list of product_review
  # metaobject references, newest first, kept in step by the syndication job.
  # `app` is the theme-app-extension alias for this app's reserved namespace.
  assign reviews_list = product.metafields.app.reviews.value
  # Same reserved namespace as the review list above. Written by the AI summary
  # job for PRO stores only; absent everywhere else, which is why the render
  # below is guarded rather than conditional on any plan flag reaching Liquid.
  assign review_summary = product.metafields.app.summary.value
  # The 1–5 histogram behind the breakdown panel, written by the aggregate job
  # from counts it already had. Absent on a store that has not resynced since
  # the field existed, which is why the panel below is guarded rather than
  # assumed.
  assign rating_breakdown = product.metafields.app.breakdown.value

  # Shop-level feature flags, written by the app when the plan changes. Liquid
  # has no way to ask what plan a store is on — there is no request to our
  # server in this path, which is the point of a server-rendered block — so
  # anything gated by plan has to arrive as data Shopify already holds.
  #
  # This decides what the form OFFERS. The proxy route decides what it ACCEPTS,
  # and that is the check that actually enforces anything: a theme edited to
  # restore the input gets a rejected upload, not a free feature.
  assign cited_features = shop.metafields.app.features.value
  assign video_enabled = false
  if cited_features.video
    assign video_enabled = true
  endif

  assign per_page = per_page | default: 10

  # `| floor`, not `| plus: 0`. The rating metafield stores scale_max as "5.0",
  # and `plus` yields the FLOAT 5.0 — which Liquid renders as "out of 5.0" and,
  # far worse, cannot be used as a range bound: `(1..5.0)` calls Integer("5.0"),
  # which raises "invalid integer". The star loop then emitted nothing at all,
  # so every rating on every storefront rendered with no stars.
  assign scale_max = 5
  if rating_field != blank and rating_field.value.scale_max != blank
    assign scale_max = rating_field.value.scale_max | floor
  endif
  if scale_max < 1
    assign scale_max = 5
  endif

  assign has_reviews = false
  if rating_count > 0
    assign has_reviews = true
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  Styles live in assets/, not in a "stylesheet" tag: that tag is a theme
  *section* feature and the extension bundler rejects it in an app block
  ("Cannot use stylesheet tag in app blocks"). Emitting the link here also
  means the stylesheet is requested before the markup below it parses, so it
  still paints without a flash of unstyled content.
{%- endcomment -%}
{{ 'cited-reviews.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

{%- comment -%}
  The merchant's palette, captured once so it can be stamped on BOTH the
  section and the rating summary.

  The summary is relocated out of this section on the embed path — it belongs
  under the product title, and the rest of the block belongs further down the
  page — and every colour here is a custom property inherited from the section
  it would be leaving. Carrying its own copy makes it self-sufficient wherever
  it lands, rather than falling back to the stylesheet defaults and quietly
  ignoring the colours the merchant chose.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- capture cited_vars -%}
--cited-star: {{ star_color }};
--cited-text: {{ text_color }};
--cited-muted: {{ muted_color }};
--cited-border: {{ border_color }};
--cited-radius: {{ corner_radius }}px;
--cited-gap: {{ spacing }}px;
{%- endcapture -%}

<section
  class="cited-reviews"
  id="cited-reviews-{{ uid }}"
  data-cited-product="{{ product.id }}"
  data-cited-per-page="{{ per_page }}"
  {% if is_embed %}
    data-cited-embed
    data-cited-anchor="{{ anchor | escape }}"
    data-cited-title="{{ product.title | escape }}"
  {% endif %}
  style="{{ cited_vars }}"
  {{ shopify_attributes }}
>
  {%- if heading != blank -%}
    <h2 class="cited-reviews__heading">{{ heading | escape }}</h2>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    ── Compact summary, for the product title ──

    EMBED PATH ONLY. This exists to be lifted out of the section and dropped
    under the product title; the section below has its own full score and
    histogram. On the app-block path nothing moves it, so rendering it here
    would put two rating displays side by side in the same section — which is
    exactly what it did.

    A disclosure, not a panel that is always open — and `details`/`summary`
    rather than a popover driven by script. The block's whole premise is that
    it renders inside Shopify's own HTML response with no JavaScript, so a
    breakdown that needs JS to open would be invisible to exactly the crawlers
    this app exists to feed. The browser gives keyboard support, focus
    handling and Escape for free.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if has_reviews and is_embed -%}
    <details class="cited-rating" data-cited-summary style="{{ cited_vars }}">
      <summary class="cited-rating__trigger">
        <span class="cited-rating__value">{{ rating_avg | round: 1 }}</span>
        {%- render 'cited-stars', rating: rating_avg, scale_max: scale_max, size: 15 -%}
        <svg class="cited-rating__caret" viewBox="0 0 10 6" width="10" height="6" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M1 1l4 4 4-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
        </svg>
        <span class="cited-rating__count">
          {%- if rating_count == 1 -%}1 review{%- else -%}{{ rating_count }} reviews{%- endif -%}
        </span>
      </summary>

      <div class="cited-rating__panel">
        <p class="cited-rating__headline">
          {%- render 'cited-stars', rating: rating_avg, scale_max: scale_max, size: 16 -%}
          <strong>{{ rating_avg | round: 1 }} out of {{ scale_max }}</strong>
        </p>
        <p class="cited-rating__total">
          {{ rating_count }} global rating{% unless rating_count == 1 %}s{% endunless %}
        </p>

        {%- render 'cited-breakdown', breakdown: rating_breakdown, total: rating_count, scale_max: scale_max -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          The way out of the panel. Without it the breakdown is a dead end —
          a shopper who opens it has decided the average is not enough and
          wants to read what people actually wrote, and on the embed path the
          reviews themselves are a long way down the page.

          A plain in-page anchor, so it works with scripting off and the back
          button behaves.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <a class="cited-rating__more" href="#cited-reviews-{{ uid }}">
          See all {{ rating_count }} review{% unless rating_count == 1 %}s{% endunless %}
          <span aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </details>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    ── Reviews section ──

    Two columns: the verdict on the left, the evidence on the right. The left
    column is what a shopper checks before deciding whether to read any further
    — score, distribution, and the invitation to add their own — and it stays
    put while they read down the list.

    The compact disclosure above is lifted out of this section entirely on the
    embed path and dropped under the product title, so this header is not a
    duplicate of it: it is the only rating display left here once that move has
    happened.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="cited-reviews__layout">
    <aside class="cited-reviews__aside">
      {%- if has_reviews -%}
        <p class="cited-reviews__score">
          {%- render 'cited-stars', rating: rating_avg, scale_max: scale_max, size: 18 -%}
          <strong>{{ rating_avg | round: 1 }} out of {{ scale_max }}</strong>
        </p>
        <p class="cited-reviews__total">
          {{ rating_count }} global rating{% unless rating_count == 1 %}s{% endunless %}
        </p>

        {%- render 'cited-breakdown', breakdown: rating_breakdown, total: rating_count, scale_max: scale_max -%}
      {%- else -%}
        <div class="cited-reviews__empty">
          <p>{{ empty_text | escape }}</p>
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        ── Review this product ──

        Signed out, this is a LINK to the login page rather than a form that
        rejects you after you have typed. Reviews require an account — a
        signed-out submission is an unverifiable claim, which makes
        "verified purchase" meaningless and one-review-per-customer
        unenforceable — so the account step is unavoidable, and the honest
        thing is to say so before the shopper invests any effort.

        `return_url` brings them back to this product afterwards.
        `routes.account_login_url`, never a hardcoded /account/login: the path
        differs on stores using new customer accounts and on any store with a
        locale prefix, where the literal path 404s or drops the shopper into
        the wrong locale.

        Signed in, it is a `details` that reveals the form in place. No
        JavaScript, no navigation, and the form is still a plain POST if the
        disclosure is opened with scripting off.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if enable_form -%}
        <div class="cited-reviews__cta">
          <p class="cited-reviews__cta-title">Review this product</p>
          <p class="cited-reviews__cta-sub">Share your thoughts with other customers</p>

          {%- unless customer -%}
            <a
              class="cited-reviews__submit cited-reviews__cta-button"
              href="{{ routes.account_login_url }}?return_url={{ product.url | url_encode }}"
            >
              {{ form_button | escape }}
            </a>
          {%- endunless -%}
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}
    </aside>

    <div class="cited-reviews__main">

  {%- comment -%}
    ── AI summary ──
    Sits between the aggregate and the review list because that is the order a
    shopper reads in: how many liked it, what they said about it, then the
    reviews themselves. Renders nothing when the metafield is absent.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if has_reviews and review_summary != blank -%}
    {%- render 'cited-summary', summary: review_summary, uid: uid -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    ── Review list ──
    Rendered server-side. Only the first page is emitted inline; further pages
    are fetched by the enhancement script. The first page is what matters for
    SEO and AI ingestion, so crawlability never depends on JS.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if reviews_list != blank -%}
    <ol class="cited-reviews__list" role="list">
      {%- for review in reviews_list limit: per_page -%}
        {%- liquid
          assign r_rating = 0
          if review.rating != blank and review.rating.value != blank
            assign r_rating = review.rating.value.rating | times: 1.0
          endif
          # `author` on the standard definition is a customer reference, not a
          # name. The display name lives in `author_display_name`.
          assign r_author = review.author_display_name | default: anonymous_label
          assign r_verified = false
          if review.app_verification_status == 'verified_buyer'
            assign r_verified = true
          endif
        -%}
        <li class="cited-review">
          {%- comment -%}
            Reading order is person → verdict → provenance → detail, which is
            how a shopper actually interrogates a review: who said this, what
            did they conclude, can I trust it, and only then the argument.
            Stars used to lead and the reviewer's name sat underneath, which
            reads as a rating that happens to have a person attached.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div class="cited-review__who">
            {%- comment -%}
              An initial rather than an avatar image: there is no photo to
              show, and a generated identicon would be decoration that costs a
              request. aria-hidden because the name follows it in full.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <span class="cited-review__avatar" aria-hidden="true">{{ r_author | slice: 0 | upcase }}</span>
            <span class="cited-review__author">{{ r_author | escape }}</span>
          </div>

          <div class="cited-review__head">
            {%- render 'cited-stars', rating: r_rating, scale_max: scale_max, size: 14 -%}
            {%- if review.title != blank -%}
              <h3 class="cited-review__title">{{ review.title | escape }}</h3>
            {%- endif -%}
          </div>

          <div class="cited-review__meta">
            {%- if review.submitted_at != blank -%}
              <time class="cited-review__date" datetime="{{ review.submitted_at | date: '%Y-%m-%d' }}">
                Reviewed on {{ review.submitted_at | date: format: 'date' }}
              </time>
            {%- endif -%}
            {%- comment -%}
              This is OUR order-match badge. It is deliberately NOT, and must
              never resemble, the "Verified by Shop" badge.

              Shopify's rule for approved review apps: use only the official
              badge assets it provides, and "do not modify, recreate, or alter
              the badge in any way." The two marks also mean different things —
              "Verified by Shop" is Shopify attesting via the Shop app, while
              this says only that Cited matched the reviewer to an order for
              this product on this store.

              So it is kept distinct on every axis a reviewer would check:
                * mark    — a bag outline, not a checkmark. A check inside a
                            circle is the shape people read as Shopify's.
                * colour  — green, distinct from all three official variants
                            (purple, black, grey).
                * wording — names the store, not Shop.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- if r_verified -%}
              <span class="cited-review__verified" title="Cited matched this reviewer to an order on this store">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="12" height="12" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                  <path
                    fill="none"
                    stroke="currentColor"
                    stroke-width="1.4"
                    stroke-linejoin="round"
                    d="M3.4 5.6h9.2l-.8 7.3a1 1 0 0 1-1 .9H5.2a1 1 0 0 1-1-.9zM6 5.6V4.3a2 2 0 0 1 4 0v1.3"
                  />
                </svg>
                Verified purchase
              </span>
            {%- endif -%}
          </div>

          {%- if review.body != blank -%}
            <div class="cited-review__body">{{ review.body | escape | newline_to_br }}</div>
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- comment -%}
            `media_urls` is the ONLY media field on the standard product_review
            definition — a bare list of URLs, with no type discriminator, and
            we cannot add a field to a definition Shopify owns. So the type is
            inferred from the URL, which is why the check below is broader than
            a file extension: Shopify's transcoded sources live under
            /cdn/shop/videos/ and carry a rendition suffix before the .mp4.

            Getting this wrong is visible rather than silent — a video URL in
            an <img> is a broken image — which is the failure mode to prefer
            over guessing wrong in the other direction.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- if show_media and review.media_urls != blank -%}
            <div class="cited-review__media">
              {%- for media_url in review.media_urls limit: 5 -%}
                {%- liquid
                  assign is_video = false
                  if media_url contains '/cdn/shop/videos/'
                    assign is_video = true
                  elsif media_url contains '.mp4'
                    assign is_video = true
                  elsif media_url contains '.webm'
                    assign is_video = true
                  elsif media_url contains '.mov'
                    assign is_video = true
                  endif
                -%}
                {%- if is_video -%}
                  {%- comment -%}
                    No poster attribute: the standard field gives us one URL per
                    item, so there is no thumbnail to pair with this source.
                    `#t=0.1` asks the browser for the frame a tenth of a second
                    in, which is what makes the player show the clip instead of
                    a black rectangle. `preload="metadata"` keeps that cheap —
                    a review list must not pull down several megabytes of video
                    nobody pressed play on.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  <video
                    class="cited-review__media-item cited-review__media-item--video"
                    src="{{ media_url }}#t=0.1"
                    controls
                    playsinline
                    preload="metadata"
                    aria-label="Customer video from a review by {{ r_author | escape }}"
                  ></video>
                {%- else -%}
                  <img
                    class="cited-review__media-item"
                    src="{{ media_url }}"
                    alt="Customer photo from a review by {{ r_author | escape }}"
                    loading="lazy"
                    decoding="async"
                    width="88"
                    height="88"
                  >
                {%- endif -%}
              {%- endfor -%}
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- if review.merchant_reply != blank -%}
            <div class="cited-review__reply">
              <span class="cited-review__reply-label">{{ shop.name | escape }} replied</span>
              <p>{{ review.merchant_reply | escape | newline_to_br }}</p>
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}
        </li>
      {%- endfor -%}
    </ol>

    {%- if rating_count > per_page -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Enhancement target. Server-rendered as a plain link to the product
        page so it still works with JS disabled; the script upgrades it to
        in-place pagination.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <a
        class="cited-reviews__more"
        href="{{ product.url }}#cited-reviews-{{ uid }}"
        data-cited-more
      >
        {{ load_more_text | escape }}
      </a>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    ── Write a review ──

    A plain HTML form posting to the app proxy on this shop's own domain. No
    JavaScript, no iframe, no third-party origin: it submits, the server
    answers with a rendered page in this theme, and it behaves identically
    with scripting disabled. The proxy path must match `subpath` in
    shopify.app.toml — Liquid cannot read that file.

    A signed-in shopper's name and email are prefilled from their account, and
    only that path can earn a "Verified purchase" badge: the app looks the
    address up from Shopify rather than believing this form. A typed address
    is an unauthenticated claim and is treated as one.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Only for a signed-in shopper. Signed out, the invitation lives in the aside
    above and is a link to the login page — saying so before any effort is
    invested beats rejecting a filled-in form.

    A `details` rather than a form that is always open: the write form is the
    largest thing in the block and almost every visitor is here to read, not to
    write. Collapsed by default it stops dominating a section whose job is to
    show what other people said. `open` is not set, so the server's HTML is the
    collapsed state and no layout shift happens on load.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if enable_form and customer -%}
    <details class="cited-reviews__form-wrap" id="cited-write-{{ uid }}">
      <summary class="cited-reviews__form-heading">{{ form_heading | escape }}</summary>

      <form
        class="cited-reviews__form"
        method="post"
        action="/apps/cited/reviews"
        accept-charset="UTF-8"
        enctype="multipart/form-data"
        data-cited-form
      >
        <input type="hidden" name="product_id" value="{{ product.id }}">
        <input type="hidden" name="return_path" value="{{ product.url }}">

        {%- comment -%}
          ── Rating ──

          Real stars the shopper clicks, built from a radio group rather than
          script: the inputs are visually hidden but still focusable, and each
          label is the star itself. `row-reverse` puts 1 on the left while the
          DOM runs 5→1, which is what lets `input:checked ~ label` light up the
          chosen star and every one below it with no JavaScript at all.

          A click-to-rate strip driven by JS would look the same and submit
          nothing with scripting off.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <fieldset class="cited-rate">
          <legend class="cited-rate__legend">Your rating</legend>
          <div class="cited-rate__stars">
            {%- for offset in (1..5) -%}
              {%- assign value = 6 | minus: offset -%}
              <input
                class="cited-rate__input"
                type="radio"
                id="cited-rate-{{ uid }}-{{ value }}"
                name="rating"
                value="{{ value }}"
                required
              >
              <label class="cited-rate__label" for="cited-rate-{{ uid }}-{{ value }}">
                <span class="cited-rate__sr">{{ value }} star{% if value != 1 %}s{% endif %}</span>
                <svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" width="26" height="26" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                  <path d="M10 1.6l2.6 5.2 5.8.85-4.2 4.1 1 5.75L10 14.8l-5.2 2.7 1-5.75L1.6 7.65l5.8-.85z"/>
                </svg>
              </label>
            {%- endfor -%}
          </div>
        </fieldset>

        {%- comment -%}
          One box, not two. A separate title field asks the shopper to
          summarise something they have not written yet, and it is the field
          people leave blank or fill with "Good".
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <label class="cited-reviews__field">
          <span class="cited-reviews__field-label">Your review</span>
          <textarea
            name="body"
            rows="3"
            maxlength="5000"
            required
            placeholder="What did you think? How did it work out for you?"
          ></textarea>
        </label>

        {%- comment -%}
          Photos are optional and go to the merchant's own Shopify Files. The
          input accepts images only and is capped server-side at four; the
          attribute here is a courtesy to the file picker, not the limit.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if show_media -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            The native file input renders as "Browse… No files selected" — an
            OS-styled control that ignores every theme setting, spans whatever
            width it likes and reads as a form from 1998 next to the rest of
            this block.

            So the input is visually hidden but left in the DOM, focusable and
            fully functional: it is a real <input type="file"> inside its
            <label>, so clicking the tile opens the picker with no JavaScript
            and keyboard users tab straight to it. The tile is decoration over
            a working control, not a replacement for one.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <label class="cited-reviews__field cited-reviews__photos">
            <span class="cited-reviews__field-label">Photos <span class="cited-reviews__optional">optional</span></span>
            <span class="cited-upload">
              <input
                class="cited-upload__input"
                type="file"
                name="photos"
                accept="image/jpeg,image/png,image/webp,image/gif"
                multiple
              >
              <span class="cited-upload__tile">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                  <rect x="3" y="5" width="18" height="14" rx="2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/>
                  <circle cx="8.5" cy="10" r="1.6" fill="currentColor"/>
                  <path d="M4 17l4.5-4.5 3 3L15 12l5 5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                </svg>
              </span>
              <small class="cited-upload__hint">Up to 4 images, 5 MB each.</small>
            </span>
          </label>

          {%- comment -%}
            One video, separate input. A single `accept` list mixing images and
            video would let someone pick four clips and have three silently
            dropped server-side, which reads as the upload being broken.

            `video/quicktime` is not optional: it is what iOS sends for a .mov,
            so leaving it out rejects most phone uploads.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- if video_enabled -%}
          <label class="cited-reviews__field cited-reviews__video">
            <span class="cited-reviews__field-label">Video <span class="cited-reviews__optional">optional</span></span>
            <span class="cited-upload">
              <input
                class="cited-upload__input"
                type="file"
                name="video"
                accept="video/mp4,video/quicktime,video/webm"
              >
              <span class="cited-upload__tile">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                  <rect x="3" y="6" width="12" height="12" rx="2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/>
                  <path d="M15 10.5l5-2.5v8l-5-2.5z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                </svg>
              </span>
              <small class="cited-upload__hint">One video, up to 50 MB. It may take a minute to appear.</small>
            </span>
          </label>
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          The shopper is never asked who they are. Shopify already knows, and
          the app resolves the name and address server-side from the customer
          id the proxy signs — which is the only version of it worth trusting.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          Only when there is actually a name to show. A Shopify customer can
          have neither first_name nor name — an account created at checkout
          with just an email has both blank — and this rendered as a dangling
          "Posting as" with nothing after it, which reads like a bug to the
          shopper and tells them nothing either way.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- assign posting_as = customer.first_name | default: customer.name -%}
        {%- if posting_as != blank -%}
          <p class="cited-reviews__as">
            Posting as <strong>{{ posting_as | escape }}</strong>
          </p>
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          Honeypot. Hidden from people by CSS and from screen readers by
          aria-hidden, left visible to the sort of bot that fills every input
          it can find. Rejected server-side.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="cited-reviews__hp" aria-hidden="true">
          <label>Website<input type="text" name="website" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off"></label>
        </div>

        <div class="cited-reviews__actions">
          <button class="cited-reviews__submit" type="submit">{{ form_button | escape }}</button>
          <p class="cited-reviews__status" data-cited-status role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
        </div>
      </form>
    </details>
  {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{%- comment -%}
  Submit without a reload. Loaded only when the form is on the page, and
  purely additive: the form works as a plain POST without it.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if enable_form and customer -%}
  <script src="{{ 'cited-reviews-form.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  ── Structured data ──
  Emitted only when reviews actually exist. Emitting AggregateRating with zero
  reviews is a Google structured-data violation and a fast route to a manual
  action — which for a merchant means a 1-star app review (PLAN.md §5.4.5).

  @id is pinned to the canonical product URL so search engines MERGE this with
  the theme's existing Product node rather than treating it as a second,
  competing product entity.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if has_reviews and enable_jsonld -%}
  {%- capture cited_jsonld -%}
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Product",
    "@id": {{ shop.url | append: product.url | json }},
    "name": {{ product.title | json }},
    "aggregateRating": {
      "@type": "AggregateRating",
      "ratingValue": {{ rating_avg | round: 2 | json }},
      "reviewCount": {{ rating_count | json }},
      "bestRating": {{ scale_max | json }},
      "worstRating": 1
    }
    {%- if reviews_list != blank -%}
    ,"review": [
      {%- for review in reviews_list limit: 5 -%}
        {%- liquid
          assign r_rating = 0
          if review.rating != blank and review.rating.value != blank
            assign r_rating = review.rating.value.rating | times: 1.0
          endif
        -%}
        {
          "@type": "Review",
          "reviewRating": {
            "@type": "Rating",
            "ratingValue": {{ r_rating | round: 1 | json }},
            "bestRating": {{ scale_max | json }},
            "worstRating": 1
          },
          "author": {
            "@type": "Person",
            "name": {{ review.author_display_name | default: 'Anonymous' | json }}
          }
          {%- if review.body != blank -%}
          ,"reviewBody": {{ review.body | json }}
          {%- endif -%}
          {%- if review.submitted_at != blank -%}
          ,"datePublished": {{ review.submitted_at | date: '%Y-%m-%d' | json }}
          {%- endif -%}
        }{%- unless forloop.last -%},{%- endunless -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    ]
    {%- endif -%}
  }
  {%- endcapture -%}
  <script type="application/ld+json">{{ cited_jsonld }}</script>
{%- endif -%}
